  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Using Community Health Services to Support Universal Health Coverage in Kenya</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project begins in Kisumu, Kenya, where rural communities face significant healthcare challenges, including limited access to quality services for chronic diseases like malnutrition, diabetes, and hypertension. Through digitizing Primary Care Networks and implementing home-based care services, the project aims to address these challenges. By training Community Health Promoters and Community Health Entrepreneurs on non-communicable disease detection and screening, the project empowers frontline healthcare workers to provide timely interventions. Integrating digital infrastructure into Primary Care Networks enables efficient patient management, including telehealth consultations and electronic medical records.This project contributes to the combi-track by integrating digital health technologies with community-based healthcare delivery models, ultimately improving access and outcomes for underserved populations. By leveraging telehealth services and eDispensing solutions, the project facilitates cost-effective care delivery, reducing healthcare costs and hospital admissions.The primary goal of the project is to enhance healthcare access, quality, and affordability for rural communities in Kisumu. Through capacity building, infrastructure development, and digital innovation, the project ensures timely access to healthcare services and improves health outcomes. By addressing the lack of accessible and affordable healthcare, particularly for chronic diseases, the project aims to create a sustainable healthcare model that can be replicated in other regions facing similar challenges.</narrative>
  </description>
